.TH HUM 1 "August 2025" "hum 0.32.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hum \- the AI stack on a biodiverse agentic kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hum
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.PP
.B hum help
[\fICOMMAND\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hum
is the user-facing command-line interface to the hum agentic stack: a daemon
(\fBhumd\fR) that hosts a mesh of cooperating agents (bees) over a native
vibration protocol called \fBthrum\fR (v0.8.0). It manages hives, bees, a
persistent event log, a peer mesh, and the daemon lifecycle.
.PP
The core model:
.TS
l l.
hive	the kind; a directory with an Orchfile, installed on a humd
bee	a participant; anything that lands on a humd, understood through its hive
nestler	a bee mid-handshake, awaiting acceptance of its hello
nestled	a bee registered on a humd
nest	the cells a hive hosts, under limits from the hive's own config
thrum	the hum-native vibration protocol; carries tones across a range of chi
petal	one unit of content (text, image, tool call, or result)
bloom	one turn of conversation, opened by a prompt, closed when it wilts
ensemble	the mesh of cooperating humds
.TE
.PP
With no command, \fBhum\fR prints a health summary (version plus daemon state).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Daemon + config + service-manager state: humd binary path and version,
identity key, peers and config files, and per-service status.
.TP
.B logs [\-n \fIN\fR]
Tail recent daemon logs (cross-platform). Default 200 lines. On macOS these
come from launchd; on Linux, journalctl.
.TP
.B doctor
One-shot full diagnostic dump. Run this first when something is wrong:
versions, config, environment sanity, the claude binary, every bee and
service state, and recent daemon + nurse logs with warnings highlighted.
Paste the output into a bug report.
.TP
.B hive
Manage the hives this humd understands. A directory with an Orchfile is a
hive. A bee naming a hive that is not installed here is refused.
.RS
.B hum hive \-\-list
The catalogue, what is installed here, and how many bees each has entered.
.br
.B hum hive \fIref\fR install
Record the hive on this humd, then run its \fBhive-install\fR unit, if
it has one, with \fBHUM_SRC\fR, \fBHUM_HIVE\fR and \fBhum\fR on its PATH; a
failed unit takes the record back. hum starts nothing else: what the hive
runs is its own business. \fIref\fR is a bundled name (e.g. \fBclaude-cli\fR,
\fBpaid-oracle\fR), a local path, or the source URL a bee
advertises (a github tree URL of a \fBhives/<hive>\fR directory).
.br
.B hum hive \fIref\fR uninstall
Forget the hive. What it started is its own to stop; a running humd
keeps understanding it until restart.
.RE
.TP
.B bee
A bee is a key its hive entered. Whatever signs a hello with that key is
the bee; humd admits only a signed hello from an entered bee whose hive is
installed. hum starts no process.
.RS
.B hum bee \-\-list
Bees entered here, which have nestled, and what humd advised each.
.br
.B hum bee enter \-\-hive \fIhive\fR \-\-role \fIrole\fR [\-\-as \fIlabel\fR]
Sign a bee in under the hive for its one role (forager, nurse, builder
or attendant) and print its id. With a label, the same hive and label
are always the same bee, so a process enters itself each time it starts
and a runtime can hold one bee per label; without one, every call mints
a new bee. Entering a label for another role files it under a new key.
.br
.B hum bee link \-\-hive \fIhive\fR \-\-as \fIlabel\fR \-\-role \fIrole\fR
Be that bee's client on stdio: enter it for its role, sign its hello, keep it
nestled through humd restarts, and pass its tones over two pipes, one
JSON object per line. A bee's thrum client in TS, Python, Go or C runs
this for it; WIRE.md says what the lines are.
.br
.B hum bee \fIid\fR exit
Sign the bee out; humd refuses its next hello.
.br
.B hum bee \fIid\fR reenter
Sign it back in.
.RE
.TP
.B comb [\fIhive\fR]
Show the comb, the kernel's ledger of what crossed it: how many tones
of each chi, and nectar (tokens in, tokens out, cache reads) per hive.
With a hive name, show only that hive's measures.
.TP
.B thehum \fIVERB\fR
Inspect the persistent chi log \(em a hash-chained, signed event log.
.RS
.B thehum status
\(em print dir, file count, total seq, latest snapshot height + timestamp.
.br
.B thehum tail [\-n \fIN\fR]
\(em tail the most recent daily file as compact JSON, one event per line
(default 20).
.br
.B thehum range \-\-author \fIhid\fR \-\-from \fIseq\fR [\-\-to \fIseq\fR]
\(em filter events by author hid and seq range (inclusive bounds).
.br
.B thehum verify
\(em check the hash chain and signatures across the whole log.
.br
.B thehum replay
\(em replay the log, counting events by chi kind.
.RE
.TP
.B ensemble
Inspect or edit the peer-mesh state.
.RS
.B hum ensemble
\(em show our identity, reach, and configured peers.
.br
.B hum ensemble peer add \fIhumd_id\fR [\-\-hint \fIHINT\fR]... [\-\-alias \fIname\fR]
\(em append a peer to peers.json. \fIhumd_id\fR is hex (with or without the
\fBhumd_\fR prefix). Repeated \fB\-\-hint\fR flags accumulate; hints are
\fBtcp:host:port\fR or \fBiroh:<64-hex>\fR. An existing entry with the same
humd_id is replaced (idempotent). Alias enables \fBhum://<alias>/path\fR
URI resolution.
.br
.B hum ensemble peer rm \fItarget\fR
\(em drop all entries matching \fItarget\fR (a humd_id hex or alias).
.RE
.TP
.B config
Show the paths hum keeps, and the hives installed here.
.TP
.B uninstall
Stop the service and remove the humd binary. State is preserved.
.TP
.B update [\-\-force] [\-\-edge]
Check for a newer release and self-update. Compares the local version
against GitHub's latest release; if newer, re-runs the canonical install
(which bounces the service atomically). \fB\-\-force\fR reinstalls even when
versions match. \fB\-\-edge\fR installs the latest main HEAD instead of the
latest release tag.
.TP
.B help
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.PP
Paths follow the XDG Base Directory spec. The defaults below assume
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR and \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR are
unset.
.TP
.B ~/.config/hum/hum.json
Main configuration (see \fBhum.schema.json\fR in the repo).
.TP
.B ~/.config/hum/peers.json
Bootstrap peers humd dials on boot; edited via \fBhum ensemble peer\fR.
.TP
.B ~/.config/hum/hives.d/
One record per installed hive: its Orchfile, as installed.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/bees/
One key per bee, filed under its id: a raw 32-byte Ed25519 seed (chmod 600).
Whoever can read a bee's key is that bee.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/bees.d/
One entry per entered bee: its hive, its public key, and whether it has exited.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/humd.key
The humd identity: a 32-byte Ed25519 seed minted on install (chmod 600).
HumdId is the sha256 of the humd's Ed25519 public key.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/thrum.sock
The thrum socket humd binds and bees connect to.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/runtime.json
Rendezvous file: the running daemon publishes its socket path, pid and
version here.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/bees.json
Live bee manifest snapshot, written on every register and disconnect.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/revoked.json
Withdrawn bee identities; checked at hello time, so a revoked bee is
refused the socket. Edited with \fBhumctl revoke\fR, a separate binary.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/routing.json
Kademlia routing table, restored on boot.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/hum/thehum/
The persistent chi log, one NDJSON file per day plus seq.bin and
snapshots/.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/run/hum/comb.json
The comb. Lifetime measures. Under the runtime root, so a reboot clears
them.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/hum/src/
Source checkout the installer builds from.
.TP
.B ~/.local/bin/
Installed binaries: \fBhum\fR, \fBhumd\fR, \fBhumctl\fR, \fBorchd\fR.
hum never calls \fBorchd\fR; it is installed as a supervisor hives may use
for their own processes, as the bundled hives do.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HUM_BIN
Path to the humd binary; overrides the default resolved location.
.TP
.B HUM_CLI
Path to the hum binary a bee's thrum client runs as its link; otherwise
\fBhum\fR on \fBPATH\fR, then \fB~/.local/bin/hum\fR.
.TP
.B HUM_THRUM_SOCK
Path to the thrum socket. Overrides the default, for both the daemon
(binds it) and clients (connect to it).
.TP
.B HUM_SOCKET
Same as \fBHUM_THRUM_SOCK\fR, checked second.
.TP
.B HUM_TRUST_REMOTE_NURSES
Set to \fB1\fR, \fBtrue\fR or \fByes\fR to let humd forward a prompt to a peer
on the strength of that peer's advertised model. Off by default: an
unverified claim hands the prompt to whoever made it. The setting
also lives in \fBhum.json\fR as \fBhumd.trustRemoteNurses\fR, which is
where it belongs: the installer never rewrites hum.json, so the gate
does not shut under you. This variable overrides that key.
.TP
.B HUM_TRUST_REMOTE_ATTENDANTS
Set to \fB1\fR, \fBtrue\fR or \fByes\fR to hand this humd's blooms the
tools a peer's attendants serve, and send a reach for one to that peer.
Off by default: the peer sees the reach's input, and its tool
descriptions reach every model here. The setting also lives in
\fBhum.json\fR as \fBhumd.trustRemoteAttendants\fR; this variable overrides
that key.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CLI_PATH
Path to the claude binary a nurse's cells spawn. Default \fBclaude\fR on
\fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the per-boot runtime files live: the thrum socket, the http
control socket, and comb.json. A reboot clears them.
.SH INSTALLATION
.PP
Install with the canonical installer:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/adiled/hum/main/install | bash
.fi
.PP
The installer builds and registers \fBhumd\fR as a user service (launchd on
macOS, systemd-style on Linux), mints the identity key, pulls in the
\fBorchd\fR supervisor binary for hives to use, and installs this page into
\fB/usr/local/share/man/man1\fR or \fB~/.local/share/man/man1\fR. Re-running
it upgrades in place.

Two channels. The default installs the latest release tag \(em what ships.
\fB\-\-edge\fR installs the latest main HEAD \(em the unreleased tip.
.RS
.B ./install
\(em build + install the latest release tag.
.br
.B ./install \-\-edge
\(em build + install the latest main HEAD.
.br
.B ./install status
\(em humd state + paths.
.br
.B ./install logs
\(em tail humd logs.
.br
.B ./install uninstall
\(em stop + remove the humd unit (state preserved).
.br
.B ./install purge
\(em remove everything including state.
.RE
.PP
After install, teach the humd its hives; each bundled hive enters its bee
and keeps it running itself:
.nf
hum hive openai-server install
hum hive paid-oracle install
hum bee --list
.fi
.SH VOCABULARY
.PP
The stack reasons in a "biodiverse" register; names carry meaning beyond
function:
.TS
l l.
thrum	bidirectional NDJSON socket between humd and any nestler
tone	one message frame on the thrum (envelope chi/rid/sid plus body)
chi	the tone's discriminator (prompt, unfurl, wilt, gossip-publish, ...)
sigil	the content-addressable hash a sid pairs under, stable across reconnects
wane	Lamport clock per sigil, incremented on every state mutation
dusk	absolute ms expiry on a tone; past dusk, drop
cell	one live LLM subprocess in a nurse's nest; fungible
brood	the state machine that walks a cell from cold to ready (PTY-only)
drone	the sentinel watching every tone (self-governance + drift detection)
forager	a bee that prompts, bringing an outside wire into thrum (HTTP/gRPC/stdio)
nurse	a bee that grows blooms in its cells (accepts prompt, emits unfurl/wilt/tendril-reach)
builder	a bee that changes the world, running what a bloom composes on its host (bash)
attendant	a bee that serves the fixed, named tools it offers, and nothing else
.TE
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBhum.schema.json\fR in the project repo, the \fBscenarios/\fR directory for
ensemble narratives, and the project website:
https://adiled.github.io/hum/.
